       A significant number of APIs have been deprecated in OpenSSL 3.0.  This
       section  describes  some  common  categories  of   deprecations.    See
       "Deprecated  function  mappings"  for  the list of deprecated functions
       that refer to these categories.

       Providers are a replacement for engines and low‐level method overrides

       Any  accessor  that   uses   an   ENGINE   is   deprecated   (such   as
       EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__sseett11__eennggiinnee(())).  Applications using engines should instead use
       providers.

       Before  providers were added algorithms were overridden by changing the
       methods used by algorithms. All these methods such as  RRSSAA__nneeww__mmeetthhoodd(())
       and  RRSSAA__mmeetthh__nneeww(())  are  now  deprecated  and can be replaced by using
       providers instead.

       Deprecated i2d and d2i functions for low‐level key types

       Any i2d and d2i functions such as dd22ii__DDHHppaarraammss(()) that take a  low‐level
       key  type  have  been  deprecated.  Applications should instead use the
       OOSSSSLL__DDEECCOODDEERR(3) and OOSSSSLL__EENNCCOODDEERR(3) APIs to read and write files.   See
       "Migration" in dd22ii__RRSSAAPPrriivvaatteeKKeeyy(3) for further details.

       Deprecated low‐level key object getters and setters

       Applications   that   set   or  get  low‐level  key  objects  (such  as
       EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__sseett11__DDHH(())  or  EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ggeett00(()))   should   instead   use   the
       OSSL_ENCODER   (See   OOSSSSLL__EENNCCOODDEERR__ttoo__bbiioo(3))   or   OSSL_DECODER  (See
       OOSSSSLL__DDEECCOODDEERR__ffrroomm__bbiioo(3))     APIs,      or      alternatively      use
       EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ffrroommddaattaa(3) or EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ttooddaattaa(3).

       Deprecated low‐level key parameter getters

       Functions  that access low‐level objects directly such as RRSSAA__ggeett00__nn(3)
       are    now    deprecated.    Applications    should    use    one    of
       EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ggeett__bbnn__ppaarraamm(3),                    EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ggeett__iinntt__ppaarraamm(3),
       l<EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ggeett__ssiizzee__tt__ppaarraamm(3)>,     EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ggeett__uuttff88__ssttrriinngg__ppaarraamm(3),
       EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ggeett__oocctteett__ssttrriinngg__ppaarraamm(3)  or EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ggeett__ppaarraammss(3) to access
       fields from an EVP_PKEY.  Gettable parameters are listed in "Common RSA
       parameters" in EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY--RRSSAA(7), "DH parameters" in EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY--DDHH(7), "DSA
       parameters" in EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY--DDSSAA(7), "FFC  parameters"  in  EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY--FFFFCC(7),
       "Common  EC  parameters"  in  EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY--EECC(7)  and "Common X25519, X448,
       ED25519 and ED448 parameters" in EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY--XX2255551199(7).  Applications  may
       also use EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ttooddaattaa(3) to return all fields.

       Deprecated low‐level key parameter setters

       Functions    that   access   low‐level   objects   directly   such   as
       RRSSAA__sseett00__ccrrtt__ppaarraammss(3) are  now  deprecated.  Applications  should  use
       EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ffrroommddaattaa(3)  to  create  new keys from user provided key data.
       Keys should be immutable once they are created, so if required the user
       may     use      EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ttooddaattaa(3),      OOSSSSLL__PPAARRAAMM__mmeerrggee(3),      and
       EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ffrroommddaattaa(3)  to  create  a  modified  key.   See "Examples" in
       EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY--DDHH(7) for more information.   See  "Deprecated  low‐level  key
       generation  functions"  for  information  on  generating  a  key  using
       parameters.

       Deprecated low‐level object creation

       Low‐level objects  were  created  using  methods  such  as  RRSSAA__nneeww(3),
       RRSSAA__uupp__rreeff(3)  and  RRSSAA__ffrreeee(3).  Applications  should  instead use the
       high‐level EVP_PKEY APIs, e.g. EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__nneeww(3), EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__uupp__rreeff(3)  and
       EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__ffrreeee(3).     See    also   EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__CCTTXX__nneeww__ffrroomm__nnaammee(3)   and
       EEVVPP__PPKKEEYY__CCTTXX__nneeww__ffrroomm__ppkkeeyy(3).

       EVP_PKEYs may be created in a variety of  ways:  See  also  "Deprecated
       low‐level  key generation functions", "Deprecated low‐level key reading
       and  writing  functions"  and  "Deprecated  low‐level   key   parameter
       setters".

       Deprecated low‐level encryption functions

       Low‐level    encryption    functions   such   as   AAEESS__eennccrryypptt(3)   and
       AAEESS__ddeeccrryypptt(3) have been informally discouraged from  use  for  a  long
       time.   Applications  should  instead  use  the  high  level  EVP  APIs
       EEVVPP__EEnnccrryyppttIInniitt__eexx(3), EEVVPP__EEnnccrryyppttUUppddaattee(3), and EEVVPP__EEnnccrryyppttFFiinnaall__eexx(3)
       or        EEVVPP__DDeeccrryyppttIInniitt__eexx(3),        EEVVPP__DDeeccrryyppttUUppddaattee(3)        and
       EEVVPP__DDeeccrryyppttFFiinnaall__eexx(3).

       Deprecated low‐level digest functions

       Use  of  low‐level  digest  functions  such  as  SSHHAA11__IInniitt(3) have been
       informally discouraged from use for a long time.   Applications  should
       instead   use   the  the  high  level  EVP  APIs  EEVVPP__DDiiggeessttIInniitt__eexx(3),
       EEVVPP__DDiiggeessttUUppddaattee(3) and EEVVPP__DDiiggeessttFFiinnaall__eexx(3), or  the  quick  one‐shot
       EEVVPP__QQ__ddiiggeesstt(3).

       Note  that  the  functions SSHHAA11(3), SSHHAA222244(3), SSHHAA225566(3), SSHHAA338844(3) and
       SSHHAA551122(3) have changed to macros that use EEVVPP__QQ__ddiiggeesstt(3).

       Deprecated low‐level signing functions

       Use of low‐level  signing  functions  such  as  DDSSAA__ssiiggnn(3)  have  been
       informally discouraged for a long time. Instead applications should use
       EEVVPP__DDiiggeessttSSiiggnn(3)      and      EEVVPP__DDiiggeessttVVeerriiffyy(3).       See     also
       EEVVPP__SSIIGGNNAATTUURREE--RRSSAA(7), EEVVPP__SSIIGGNNAATTUURREE--DDSSAA(7), EEVVPP__SSIIGGNNAATTUURREE--EECCDDSSAA(7)  and
       EEVVPP__SSIIGGNNAATTUURREE--EEDD2255551199(7).

       Deprecated low‐level MAC functions

       Low‐level   mac   functions   such   as  CCMMAACC__IInniitt(3)  are  deprecated.
       Applications should instead use the  new  EEVVPP__MMAACC(3)  interface,  using
       EEVVPP__MMAACC__CCTTXX__nneeww(3),        EEVVPP__MMAACC__CCTTXX__ffrreeee(3),        EEVVPP__MMAACC__iinniitt(3),
       EEVVPP__MMAACC__uuppddaattee(3) and EEVVPP__MMAACC__ffiinnaall(3) or the single‐shot MAC  function
       EEVVPP__QQ__mmaacc(3).    See   EEVVPP__MMAACC(3),   EEVVPP__MMAACC--HHMMAACC(7),  EEVVPP__MMAACC--CCMMAACC(7),
       EEVVPP__MMAACC--GGMMAACC(7),          EEVVPP__MMAACC--KKMMAACC(7),           EEVVPP__MMAACC--BBLLAAKKEE22(7),
